Embedded React: paint a page ScrollView's content into a separate scroll layer.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, after the patches listed before this
one there. Changed files: engine/include/native_renderer.h,
engine/include/er_scene.h, engine/scene/compositor.c, engine/rendering/gradient.c,
engine/rendering/transform.c, engine/rendering/transform.h,
backends/software/renderer_backend.c, backends/software/software_backend.h,
engine/tests/scroll/CMakeLists.txt and engine/tests/scroll/test_scroll_layer.c
(new). Not yet proposed upstream.

Scroll by copy still rasterizes the strip a scroll exposes on the scroll frame.
A backend that implements the new optional layer_resize/layer_begin/layer_end
callbacks lets its host keep one ScrollView's content, unscrolled, as a separate
image (a GPU texture); the host shows its visible window over the framebuffer
(er_scroll_layer_get), so a scroll repaints nothing. The engine paints the layer
in regions of at most 128 rows, each from scratch inside its own clip, so the
backend needs only a staging buffer of one region. It layers the largest
vertical ScrollView whose ancestors paint one opaque color behind the viewport
and over which nothing else paints; any other case keeps the existing path.
The damage pre-pass, moved into damage_node(), runs once per space. The layer
is tracked in 128-row bands: a commit paints the visible ones that are stale,
damage far from the viewport only marks bands stale, and
er_scroll_layer_prefetch() paints quiet stale bands. The software backend
implements the callbacks when er_software_enable_layer() is given a host.
test_scroll_layer stages each region in a garbage-filled buffer and compares
the composite with a full repaint without the layer after scrolls, a shadow,
gradient, scale and opacity across a region edge, content changes, layout
shifts, removed and added rows, a nested horizontal scroll, an overlay and a
refused layer.
Two paints depended on where on the framebuffer they landed, so content
painted in the layer, or moved by scroll copy, could differ by one level from
a fresh paint: gradient dithering is now keyed to the box, and the 2D transform
blit maps points relative to its source with a matrix built about the node's
own origin.

